.nh
.TH "RAIL0" "1" "Aug 2026" "rail0 CLI" ""

.SH NAME
rail0-wallets-delete - Deactivate a wallet — soft delete, reversible (by id or 0x address)


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrail0 wallets delete  [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Deactivate a wallet. Despite the verb, this is a SOFT delete and it is
reversible: the gateway sets active=false and keeps the row, so 'wallets update
 --active true' brings it back. Nothing is removed, nothing on-chain is
touched, and no funds move.

.PP
It is the same operation as 'wallets update  --active false'. Both
spellings exist because the API exposes a DELETE verb as well as the flag —
use whichever reads better where you are writing it.

.PP
What deactivation actually does: the address stops being offered by
\&'payment-methods', so buyers can no longer discover it and no new payment can
name it, and webhooks stop being dispatched for payments to it.

.PP
Existing payments still SETTLE — they carry their own payee address and the chain
does not care — but you stop being TOLD about the ones still in flight: webhook
dispatch resolves the payee wallet's account, so a deactivated wallet delivers
nothing further and no delivery is recorded as skipped. Capture them, or expect
to poll. (rail0-gateway#237)


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for delete

.PP
\fB-y\fP, \fB--yes\fP[=false]
	Skip the confirmation prompt (required to run non-interactively)


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--base-url\fP=""
	RAIL0 gateway base URL (env: RAIL0_BASE_URL)

.PP
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Print HTTP request/response details to stderr

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	Force plain (uncolored) JSON output (useful in scripts)

.PP
\fB--pretty\fP[=false]
	Use the key-value/table view instead of JSON (terminal only)

.PP
\fB-q\fP, \fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	Print only the result's id/hash, one per line (useful in scripts)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  rail0 wallets delete 0xABCD…
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBrail0-wallets(1)\fP
